<section class="wrap observe" id="about">
  <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
  <div class="about-grid">
    <div>
      <p>
        I care about how a city takes care of the people living in it. That's the short version,
        and most of what I do comes back to it one way or another.
      </p>
      <p>
        I studied political science because I wanted to understand how decisions get made and
        who they land on. The classes that stuck were the unglamorous ones — public
        administration, public finance, how a budget gets written and what it says about
        priorities. Outside class, the education came from a shelter kitchen in Lexington and
        from elementary classrooms in Fayette County, both of which teach you faster than a
        textbook that public systems are made of people having a hard day.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also build things. I taught myself to code during lockdown and I've run my own servers
        ever since. I bring it up because it's genuinely useful — I can build a tool instead of
        writing a memo asking somebody else to — not because I want to be introduced as a
        developer.
      </p>
      <p>I live in Covington now, I'm in the Mayor's Academy, and I'd like to do this work here.</p>
      <p>
        This is the start, not the whole plan. Long-term, I'm working toward the U.S. House —
        and eventually, the Speaker's chair. Not for the title. That's the seat where the actual
        language of a bill gets decided, and that language is what decides who gets help and who
        doesn't.
      </p>
    </div>
